          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as true. The surrounding comparisons amplify the asserted degree of that emotional state, making avowal the expression’s clear dominant mode, regardless of sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The occurrence claims present filial love but does not itself create, renew, or invoke a commitment by which Goneril positions herself as bound to future care, obedience, or service. Lear’s proposed transfer of territory makes the declaration consequential, but that transfer is Lear’s undertaking and response, not a promise contained in Goneril’s expression. Her reference to what a child loves characterizes the claimed emotion rather than affirming a specific obligation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within an extended, highly composed and competitive declaration rather than as a reflex-like affective discharge. Claims that speech is inadequate are part of the rhetoric and do not make the focal words an involuntary exclamation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression substantially functions as strategic praise and a competitive claim to merit Lear’s largest bounty. Lear explicitly solicits declarations in order to distribute territory, and he immediately rewards Goneril’s answer. Kent later characterizes the situation as power yielding to flattery, supporting this residual courtly-strategic function.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Of all these bounds, even from this line to this,\\nWith shadowy forests and with champains rich’d,\\nWith plenteous rivers and wide-skirted meads,\\nWe make thee lady:\"},{\"block_id\":\"later_1\",\"quotation\":\"When power to flattery bows?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public royal proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most and expressly links their answers to the size of their inheritances. Goneril answers first with an elaborate superlative avowal addressed to her father. Lear accepts it as meritorious and grants her territory.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"absent\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking by Goneril is established for this occurrence: she avows extreme filial love without committing herself to specified care, obedience, or future conduct. Lear accepts the avowal and rewards it with land, but that is uptake of her claim in the love contest, not acceptance of a promise. The supplied later discourse strongly frames the speech as flattery—Kent refers to “power to flattery” and hopes the sisters’ deeds will validate their “words of love”—while Goneril’s subsequent treatment of Lear undermines evidence of actual affection.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as William Shakespeare’s The Tragedy of King Lear, specifically Goneril’s speech in the opening love test.\",\"limitations\":\"The represented speech situation and later supplied conduct strongly support a strategic, insincere reading, but literary evidence cannot directly establish Goneril’s private emotional state with certainty. The assessment is limited to the supplied blocks and does not treat the rest of the work as absent.\"}"
        }
